          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Wick algebra
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+quantum+observables"} algebra of quantum observables
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+fields"} free
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum fields
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/harmonic+oscillators"} harmonic oscillators
            or in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+fields"} free fields
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            one encounters
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operators"} linear operators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a_k, a^\ast_k\}_{k \in K}
            that satisfy the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+commutation+relation"} canonical commutation relation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi a
                      %mi j
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mi diag
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a_i, a^\ast_j] = diag((c_k))_{i j}
            %em normal ordered polynomial
            or
            %em Wick polynomial
            (
            %a{:href => "#Wick50"}> Wick 50
            ) one means a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial"} polynomial
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo :
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} :P:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P((a_k, a^\ast_k))
            in these operators by ordering all “creation operators”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_k^\ast
            to the left of all “annihiliation operators”. For example focusing on a single mode
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            we have:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo :
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mo *
                          %mo :
                          %mo =
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo :
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mo =
                          %mi a
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo :
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mo *
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mo =
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mo *
                          %mi a
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo :
                          %mi a
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mo *
                          %mo :
                          %mo =
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mo *
                          %mi a
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo :
                          %mi a
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mo *
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mo =
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mo *
                          %mi a
                          %mi a
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi etc
                          %mo .
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \:a^\ast: = a^\ast
                  \\\
                  \:a: = a
                  \\\
                  \:a^\ast a: = a^\ast a
                  \\\
                  \:a a^\ast: = a^\ast a
                  \\\
                  \:a a^\ast a: = a^\ast a a
                  \\\
                  etc.
                  }
                  \\,
          %p
            The intuitive idea is that these operators span a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi vac
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert vac \rangle \in \mathcal{H}
            characterized by the condition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi vac
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mphantom
                    %mi AAA
                  %mtext for all
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a_k \vert vac \rangle = 0
                  \\phantom{AAA}
                  \\text{for all} \,\, k
          %p
            hence (if we think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_k
            as acting by “removing a quantum in mode
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            with vanishing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+value"} vacuum expectation value
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            they model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering"} scattering
            processes where quanta enter a reaction process (the modes corresponding to the “annihilation” operators
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_k
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi a
                      %mi k
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a^\ast_k
          %p
            The product of two Wick polynomials, computed in the ambient operator algebra and then re-expressed as a Wick polynomial, is given by computing the relevant sequence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutators"} commutators
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick%27s+lemma"} Wick's lemma
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi a
                      %mo *
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi a
                      %mo *
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mo *
                  %mi a
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mo *
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  {:a^\ast a:} \, {:a^\ast a:}
                  \=
                  \:a^\ast a^\ast a a: + \hbar \, :a^\ast a:
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar = [a, a^\ast]
            is the value of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+commutation+relations"} canonical commutator
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            thus obtained is hence called the
            %em algebra of normal ordered operators
            or
            %em Wick polynomial algebra
            or just
            = succeed "." do
              %em Wick algebra
          %p
            This plays a central role in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+fields"} free fields
            is traditionally
            %em defined
            as the corresponding Wick algebra.
          %p
            But the Wick algebra in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            may also be understood more systematically from first principles of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+deformation+quantization"} Moyal deformation quantization
            of the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls+bracket"} Peierls bracket
            modified to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+K%C3%A4hler+structure"} almost Kähler structure
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-point+function"} 2-point function
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-free+Hadamard+state"} quasi-free Hadamard state
            (
            %a{:href => "#Dito90"}> Dito 90
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DutschFredenhagen01"} Dütsch-Fredenhagen 01
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green+hyperbolic+differential+equation"} Green hyperbolic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations"} equations
            of motion, the analog of the star product tensor (
            %a{:href => "star+product#eq:InStarProductTensorInvertingHermitianForm"}> this equation
            )
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi \;=\; \tfrac{i}{2}\omega^{-1} + \tfrac{1}{2}g^{-1}
          %p
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
            according to
            %a{:href => "Hadamard+distribution#eq:DeompositionOfHadamardPropagatorOnMinkowkski"} this equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi H
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi S
                  %mo +
                  %mi H
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta_H
                  \\;=\;
                  \\tfrac{i}{2}\Delta_S + H
                  \\,.
          %p
            Understood in this form the construction directly generalizes to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory+on+curved+spacetimes"} quantum field theory on curved spacetimes
            (
            %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen95"}> Brunetti-Fredenhagen 95
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen00"} Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#HollandsWald01"} Hollands-Wald 01
          %p
            Finally, the shift by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-free+Hadamard+state"} quasi-free Hadamard state
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov deformation quantization
            (
            %a{:href => "#Collini16"}> Collini 16
            ). For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p
            Traditionally the Wick algebra is regarded as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
            acting on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fock+space"} Fock space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            on the space of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcausal+polynomial+observables"} microcausal polynomial observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} represented
            yet on a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#AbstractWickAlgebra"} Abstract Wick algebra
              \.
          %p
            That the abstract Wick algebra indeed has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+representation"} faithful representation
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fock+space"} Fock space
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick%27s+lemma"} Wick's lemma
          %p Similarly there is the
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#AbstractTimeOrderedProduct"} Abstract time-ordered product
          %p
            The traditional notation for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+products"} operator products
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fock+space"} Fock space
            may be carried across the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            map to the abstract Wick algebra:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#OperatorProductAndNormalOrderedProduct"} Operator product notation
          %p
            The abstract Wick algebra carries a canonical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+on+a+star-algebra"} state on a star-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-point+function"} 2-point function
            is just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
            that the abstract Wick algebra structure is constructed from. This we discuss in
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#HadamardVacuumStatesOnWickAlgebras"} Hadamard vacuum states
              \.
          %p
            The only non-trivial part of the proof of the
            = succeed "-property" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+on+a+star-algebra"} state
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WickAlgebraCanonicalState"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fock+space"} Fock space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            (
            %a{:href => "#Duetsch18"}> Dütsch 18, remark 2.20
            ). From the point of view of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
          %h3#AbstractWickAlgebra Abstract Wick algebra
          %p
            The abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green+hyperbolic+differential+equation"} Green hyperbolic differential equation
            is directly analogous to the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+vector+space"} Kähler vector space
            (
            %a{:href => "star+product#WickAlgebraOfAlmostKaehlerVectorSpace"}> this def.
            ) under the following identification of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
            with the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+space"} Kähler space
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
          #WightmanPropagatorAsKaehlerVectorSpaceStructure.num_remark
